## FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The audit is considered complete only when:

- All eight specified pages have been reviewed.
- Every important metric is backed by an identifiable authoritative data source.
- No mock or hardcoded values are presented as live business data.
- Frontend and backend data remain synchronized.
- Real-time events correctly update affected dashboard components.
- Revenue calculations are independently validated.
- Payment and subscription statuses are accurate.
- Administrative controls perform their intended operations.
- Customer and financial information is protected.
- All interactive tabs, buttons, filters, and reporting features work correctly.
- Critical and high-severity findings are resolved and retested.
- All remaining limitations are documented.

**MOST IMPORTANT: DO NOT JUST MAKE THE DASHBOARD LOOK FUNCTIONAL. MAKE THE UNDERLYING SYSTEM FUNCTIONALLY CORRECT, FINANCIALLY ACCURATE, SECURE, AND FULLY CONNECTED TO REAL BACKEND DATA.**

Work systematically, inspect the existing codebase before making changes, trace every data dependency, implement the necessary fixes, and verify the results through actual tests.

Do not declare completion based solely on successful builds or visually correct frontend components. A successful build is not proof of correct data, working integrations, or accurate financial reporting.

My additional recommendation
For Qguard Helix, I would add three particularly important requirements to this audit:

1. Revenue reconciliation: Your administration dashboard should be able to reconcile Supabase subscription records against PayPal/Stripe transactions. This prevents your displayed revenue from becoming inaccurate when webhooks are delayed, duplicated, or missed.

2. Entitlement synchronization: When a customer purchases, upgrades, downgrades, or cancels a subscription, their actual Qguard Messaging, Quantum Vault, and other plan-dependent entitlements should automatically reflect the correct subscription status.

3. Financial audit trail: Every administrative change involving payments, refunds, subscription modifications, and customer account status should have an auditable record of who performed it, when it happened, and what changed.

One important distinction: SSE is useful for pushing server events to dashboards, but it should not be treated as the source of truth. The database and verified backend transactions remain authoritative.